.TH TOWER 1 "" "tower @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tower \- terminal dashboard for the Tower Claude Code guard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tower
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tower
opens the Tower terminal dashboard: your location and the country the guard is
pinned to, network health, plan usage, and a live list of every Claude Code
agent running on this machine.
.PP
It also starts
.BR towerd (1)
if it is not already running, so opening the dashboard is enough to arm the
guard. The dashboard is a thin front-end: it reads
.I ~/.tower/state.json
and writes command files into
.IR ~/.tower/cmd/ .
All the logic lives in the daemon.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B c
set the target country
.TP
.B w
cycle keep-awake: off, idle, lid-closed
.TP
.B r
re-check location and network now
.TP
.B q
quit the dashboard (the daemon keeps running)
.TP
.B ?
the full key list, in the app
.SH FAIL-CLOSED
Claude requests are allowed only while Tower has confirmed both that you are
inside the target country and that there is a usable network path to Anthropic.
Anything else — checking, cached, off-country, offline, a captive portal — is
held and then answered 503, which Claude Code renders as its own
.I Retrying
spinner. A blocked request is pending, not failed, and resumes by itself when
the condition clears. Seeing a block is Tower doing its job; quitting only
removes the guard.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.tower/state.json
everything the daemon knows, rewritten once a second
.TP
.I ~/.tower/config.json
country, theme, plan size, whether routing is on
.TP
.I ~/.tower/daemon.log
.TP
.I ~/.claude/settings.json
where routing is installed, in the
.B env
block only
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR towerd (1)
